            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Physics</strong><br>\nIn 1903, Marie Curie became the first woman to win the Nobel Prize in Physics, which she shared with Pierre Curie and Henri Becquerel. They were honored for their combined work on the phenomenon of radioactivity. The Nobel Prize recognized their contribution to an emerging field that would eventually have profound implications for both science and medicine.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Discovery of Polonium and Radium</strong><br>\nMarie and Pierre Curie's work did not stop with the concept of radioactivity. They continued their experiments and in 1898, they announced the discovery of two new elements: polonium, named after Marie's native Poland, and radium. Isolating radium proved to be a difficult task, but they succeeded, which allowed for its use in developing cancer treatments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize in Chemistry</strong><br>\nIn 1911, Marie Curie was awarded her second Nobel Prize, this time in Chemistry, for her discovery of radium and polonium and for her work in the isolation of radium and the study of its chemical properties. She was the first person to receive two Nobel Prizes and remains one of the most renowned scientists in history.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
