          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>World War I and Portable X-rays</strong><br>\nDuring World War I, Marie Curie developed portable X-ray units that could be used to diagnose injuries on the battlefield. She recognized the potential for X-ray technology in medical treatment and trained other women to operate these 'radiological cars,' which significantly improved the care of wounded soldiers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Radium Institute and Legacy</strong><br>\nIn 1914, the Radium Institute, now known as the Curie Institute, was opened in Paris, where research into radioactivity could continue. After Pierre's tragic death in 1906, Marie took over his teaching post, becoming the first female professor at the Sorbonne. Her relentless work ethic and dedication to science inspired generations of researchers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Enduring Impact</strong><br>\nMarie Curie died on July 4, 1934, from aplastic anemia, a condition believed to be caused by prolonged exposure to radiation. Her legacy endures not only in the scientific community but also in the public sphere. She is remembered for her monumental contributions to science, her role as a pioneering woman in the field, and her humanitarian efforts during the war. Her work continues to influence medical treatments and scientific research to this day.</p>\n"
              },
